# Maintaining @DISPLAY@

This guide belongs to **@NAME@**. You own the generated source, configuration,
database, migration history and maintenance decisions. You may deliberately change
any initial architectural choice. No original starter checkout, shared runtime,
mandatory CLI, upstream Git synchronization or upgrade service is required.
Update your own repository through small reviewed changes; do not merge another
starter's history as an application upgrade mechanism.

Maintainers own security reporting, supported versions, monitoring, backups,
recovery testing and update scheduling. Keep the upstream MIT notice in
[LICENSE](LICENSE). Review [SECURITY.md](SECURITY.md) and configure your own private
reporting contact before accepting sensitive reports.

## Choose the update scope

- **Routine dependency update:** stays inside manifest constraints, but still needs
  lockfile review and relevant tests. Semver compatibility is not a guarantee.
- **Major upgrade:** changes constraints/APIs or compatibility requirements; read
  upstream release notes, migrate deliberately and test dependent components.
- **Toolchain upgrade:** changes compiler/build tooling, not automatically every
  library. Check dependencies' compiler and runtime requirements.
- **Security remediation:** establish affected versions, dependency paths, vendor
  fixes and reachability before choosing the smallest compatible correction.
- **Application release:** deploys a tested source/image revision; dependency updates
  and schema changes must be assessed before replacement.

Do not combine unrelated upgrades or automatically apply forced audit fixes. Record
what changed, why, tests actually executed and residual risks in your own PRs.

