[Application CI](.github/workflows/ci.yml) reads the Rust toolchain and Node version
files. Its Node installation supplies a bundled npm; the security job separately
installs the exact audited npm version. `packageManager` records a selection but does
not itself install npm. Check `node --version` and `npm --version` locally. Scanner
version mismatches fail rather than silently changing enforcement.

## Offline consistency check

From this project's root, with Python 3.11+ only:

```sh
python3 scripts/check-toolchain-pins.py
python3 scripts/test-toolchain-pins.py -v
```

The read-only checker runs without network, Rust, Node or Docker. CI runs it before
installing frontend dependencies or compiling code. It selects this application's
own files; it does not compare your versions with an upstream starter. Exit 0 means
the checked relationships agree; nonzero prints affected files, observed values
and correction hints. Tests mutate disposable declaration copies, not project files.

Update the selected Rust file or Node file first, then the related Docker/installer
and metadata declarations in the version map. For scanners, start with
scripts/security-tools.json and coordinate the manifest-driven installer, current
scan-guide introduction and any explicit npm CI installation. Resolve a legitimate
mismatch by updating/reviewing the related files, not by automatically rewriting
pins or weakening checks. Intentional workflow/installer redesigns may require a
small update to the narrowly targeted extraction rules.

Checks cover selected Rust versus Docker/explicit Cargo/security-install compiler,
MSRV no higher than the selected compiler, exact Node pins versus Docker/CI readers,
selected Node membership in manifest/lockfile engine ranges, npm metadata/security
installation, package identities, scanner installer selections/locked installation,
Trivy checksum format/verification wiring, current scan-guide version claims and
explicit current Rust/Node setup instructions when present in README/CONTRIBUTING.
Initial dated snapshots in this guide are historical and are not enforced as current
version requirements. SQLx registry declarations need a registry lock entry; if
SQLx uses a full Git revision, its lock source must agree;
a future published dependency is permitted. SQLite binding resolutions and the
optional minimatch override are reported, not fixed forever by the checker.

MSRV is a floor, not an exact compiler pin. An engine range can permit several Node
versions. Cargo constraints need not equal resolved lockfile versions. This checker
does not resolve Cargo/npm dependencies, prove lockfile freshness for every library,
validate a replacement SQLx release, or require temporary overrides. Stable numeric
pins and common Node comparator, OR, caret, tilde, wildcard and numeric hyphen ranges
are supported; unsupported range syntax is reported explicitly rather than guessed.
It is not a complete npm-semver/YAML/shell parser. CI readers/installer expressions
are checked for the known project layout, not arbitrary workflow semantics.

A 64-hex checksum declaration is **not** proof that the checksum belongs to the
selected release: verify authenticity against official release metadata separately.
The checker never downloads an archive or runs an installer/scanner. It cannot prove
Docker digest provenance, MSRV build compatibility, dependency security or successful
deployment. Continue running the appropriate builds, tests and audits below.

## Rust toolchain and libraries

Run native Cargo commands from `backend`, so rustup selects its pinned toolchain.
A C compiler/linker is required for bundled SQLite. See [README](README.md) for setup.

Selected Rust is the compiler used for development/builds. `rust-version` declares
an MSRV, not necessarily the same patch release. Testing only on the selected
compiler does not prove the full MSRV/target support matrix.

For a compiler update:

1. Read compiler/dependency release notes and target support. Choose an explicit
   version in `backend/rust-toolchain.toml`; keep rustfmt/Clippy components.
2. Update the Rust builder tag **and verified digest**, its explicit Cargo toolchain
   selection in Dockerfile, and the installer toolchain in security-tools.json.
   Updating only rust-toolchain.toml leaves those other selections unchanged.
3. Decide whether to change the declared MSRV. Test it separately if claiming support.
4. Install the selected compiler with rustup, then run the checks below and rebuild
   the production image when packaging/toolchain changes.

Cargo.toml contains allowed dependency constraints and features; Cargo.lock records
exact resolved versions and Git revisions. Commit reviewed lockfile changes with
manifest changes. Do not delete a lockfile just to make a failed build pass.

From `backend`, inspect the graph and preview a focused compatible update:

```sh
cargo tree --locked
cargo update -p axum --dry-run
```

The preview is not an update or a security assessment. For an intentional compatible
update, `cargo update -p axum` changes the lockfile within existing constraints.
For a major upgrade, edit the chosen constraint/features in Cargo.toml, resolve the
lockfile with Cargo, adapt code, and inspect the resulting dependency diff. Do not
use `--locked` for the deliberate resolution step; use it for subsequent validation:

```sh
cargo fmt --all -- --check
cargo check --locked
cargo clippy --locked --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings
cargo test --locked
cargo build --locked
```

Run RustSec from the project root as described in the security section. A successful
compile alone does not establish migration or runtime compatibility.

## SQLx, bundled SQLite and migration history

The initial [manifest](backend/Cargo.toml) pins official upstream SQLx commit
`10462fa4414c6c7f1ed1d39bb31678e3b726878c`. This temporary immutable Git pin allows
`libsqlite3-sys` 0.39.x; the initial lock resolves 0.39.1, bundling SQLite 3.54.1.
It addresses RUSTSEC-2026-0459, whose patched binding range starts at 0.39.0.

The published SQLx 0.9.0 examined during remediation limited the binding to
`>=0.30.1, <0.38.0`. Blindly replacing the Git pin with that release would lose the
compatible patched range. Do not assume a newer fixed release exists: inspect the
release's actual constraints, feature selection, MSRV and advisory status first.
The pinned commit includes upstream changes beyond tag 0.9.0, so assess API behavior
as well as the SQLite constraint when replacing it.

From `backend`, check the actual resolution:

```sh
cargo tree --locked -i libsqlite3-sys
cargo tree --locked -e features
```

Keep the narrow SQLite features unless deliberately reviewed: bundled SQLite,
unlock notification, migration and macros. Do not accidentally enable extension
loading/deserialization through a broader feature. Do not force an incompatible
binding or substitute an unreviewed fork merely to clear an audit.

Replace the temporary pin only when the selected upstream release supports a
patched binding and the application's API/MSRV requirements. Review Cargo.lock,
run RustSec and the native tests, then repeat Docker, migration and recovery checks.
The backend bundles SQLite; the runtime `sqlite3` CLI is a separate OS package used
for recovery. Assess **both** when changing SQLite or runtime packages.

[Migration files](backend/migrations) are embedded at compile time. Add a new
versioned file; never silently edit applied migrations or delete migration records.
Preserve SQLx checksums and test both fresh databases and retained existing data.
[build.rs](backend/build.rs) watches migration changes so the binary is rebuilt.
Back up and verify recovery before schema changes. Destructive changes need a
separate reviewed plan; startup migrations do not provide automatic downgrade.

## Node.js, npm and frontend libraries

Node.js and npm are the chosen frontend toolchain, used to develop and compile
assets. Production serves those assets through Rust; no Node process is required.

For a Node update, read the engine requirements of Vite, plugins and test tools.
Update frontend/.node-version, package.json engines and the Node Docker builder
(tag and verified digest) together. Coordinate `@types/node` when appropriate.
For npm, update packageManager, security-tools.json and the explicit npm installation
in security CI. Select/install the intended npm in your user-managed Node environment
and confirm the version before running audits; do not disable TLS verification.

From `frontend`, inspect available updates with `npm outdated`. Its nonzero exit can
mean outdated packages, not infrastructure failure. For an intentional compatible
React update, `npm update react react-dom` resolves within existing constraints and
updates installed packages/lockfile. Major React upgrades may require coordinated
React DOM and type packages. Vite upgrades also require checking its React/Tailwind
plugins, Node engines and Vitest compatibility. TypeScript changes need typechecking
and test review. Choose versions from upstream release notes rather than a blind
"latest" update.

Edit package.json deliberately for changed constraints, then run `npm install` with
the selected npm to resolve package-lock.json. Review both files. Afterwards verify
that a locked clean install works; do not hand-edit integrity entries or use forced
`npm audit fix` as the upgrade plan:

```sh
npm ci --no-audit --no-fund
npm run typecheck
npm run lint
npm test
npm run build
```

The initial `minimatch` override is 11.0.5, introduced for development-tooling advisory
GHSA-g3hv-75wg-m249. This version requires compatible Node tooling. Reassess with
`npm explain minimatch` and current upstream dependency requirements/advisories.
Remove the override only when parent tools resolve compatible patched versions
without it; regenerate the lockfile and repeat locked install, typecheck, lint,
tests, build and both npm audit scopes. Do not assume a clean audit alone proves
that removing the override is compatible.

## Docker and runtime package maintenance

The [Dockerfile](Dockerfile) initially pins Node 24.22.1/bookworm-slim and Rust
1.99.1/slim-bookworm builders, and Ubuntu 26.04 LTS runtime, all by digest. Review
publisher release/security notices and verify the selected tag/digest and target
architectures using the publisher's official registry. A tag can move; a digest
selects content. Change the reviewed tag and matching digest together. Do not copy
an unverified digest from a third-party recommendation.

APT installs/upgrades against live Ubuntu repositories. Pinning the base digest
therefore does **not** guarantee identical installed packages on later builds.
Rebuild to obtain available package updates and scan the actual final candidate.
When validating package refreshes, use a clean build so a cached APT layer does not
hide changes. From the generated project root:

```sh
docker build --no-cache -t @NAME@:maintenance-check .
docker compose config --quiet
python3 scripts/docker-smoke.py --image @NAME@:maintenance-check -v
python3 scripts/sqlite-backup-smoke.py --image @NAME@:maintenance-check -v
python3 scripts/security-scan.py image --project . --image @NAME@:maintenance-check
```

Build once and reuse the candidate for these tests/scans; registry access, Docker
BuildKit and Compose v2 are required. In a trusted TLS-proxy environment the Dockerfile
also supports a public CA through the `proxy_ca` BuildKit secret, as explained in
the scan guide; never turn off certificate verification.
For changes not involving OS refresh, normal build caching may be used deliberately.

Retain UID/GID 10001, read-only runtime compatibility, writable local `/data` storage,
one SQLite instance and serialized replacement, health checks and graceful shutdown.
The current Compose grace period is 15 seconds; shutdown timeout defaults to 10.
Do not assume unhealthy status triggers automatic restart. Base/package changes must
retain helper compatibility, ownership, certificates and the ability to back up and
restore. Consult [release/recovery runbook](#releases-updates-and-recovery).

### GNU recovery decision and residual uutils risk

Recovery helpers explicitly use `/usr/bin/gnudirname`, `/usr/bin/gnurealpath`,
`/usr/bin/gnumktemp`, `/usr/bin/gnurm`, `/usr/bin/gnusync` and `/usr/bin/gnuln`.
They check executability/GNU identity before accessing data, rather than falling
back to PATH or default uutils commands. These names are supplied by the selected
Ubuntu runtime; another host/image must supply compatible tools or helpers fail.
This changes maintenance command selection, not normal Rust startup.

A provider replacement investigation was stopped: a safe supported transition was
not established within the constraints. `coreutils` is essential, and installed
`coreutils-from-uutils` is protected and requires rust-coreutils. GNU is already
installed, but ordinary tested APT replacement plans failed; the explicit removal
plan requested an essential/protected removal override. Do not force removals,
edit package status, or manually replace package-managed executables.

The uutils package remains installed and its vulnerabilities were **not** eliminated.
Explicit GNU recovery commands reduce operational use, not scanner inventory.
Track future vendor fixes or a supported transition separately with fresh evidence.
Do not treat a vendor severity change or absence of a fix as proof of safety.

## Security tools and findings

See [SECURITY-SCANNING.md](SECURITY-SCANNING.md) for installation, exact scan commands,
report handling and exceptions. The project includes its own installer, pins,
wrapper and [workflow](.github/workflows/security.yml). No original checkout is needed.
With pinned tools installed/on PATH, run from this project's root:

```sh
python3 scripts/security-scan.py rust --project .
python3 scripts/security-scan.py npm --project .
```

The npm wrapper checks all and production-only graphs. Use the image command above
only after building the image. Direct `npm audit` is useful for investigation but
its default exit policy differs from this project's production-only enforcement.
RustSec checks every lockfile vulnerability; informational warnings are visible
separately. npm blocks production HIGH/CRITICAL; image scanning blocks HIGH/CRITICAL
even without a fix. Other findings remain reportable. Exit 0 means completed and
nonblocking, 1 means blocking findings, 2 means incomplete/infrastructure failure.
A green result is not proof that the application is secure.

For a scanner update, independently verify the official release/version and trusted
checksums. Update security-tools.json; for Trivy also verify/replace its Linux/amd64
archive SHA-256 against the official release metadata before installation. The
installer verifies that checksum. cargo-audit installation uses an exact version
and `--locked`; review upstream provenance and dependency changes as well. Update
any explicit workflow install versions (notably npm), guide snapshots and relevant
regressions. Keep GitHub Actions pinned to reviewed commits. Run the copied policy
regressions and fresh scans, including failure cases:

```sh
python3 scripts/test-security-scan.py -v
```

The download installer supports Trivy Linux/amd64; other hosts need a verified
installation of the pinned scanner. Workflow artifact upload and optional Code
Scanning depend on your repository settings; review triggers/default branch and
permissions. Configure your own reporting contact and supported releases.

For unresolved findings, record date, scanner/database, advisory/package/version,
dependency path or OS family, runtime use/reachability evidence, vendor backport/fix
status, owner, decision and reassessment condition/date in your own maintenance
record. Package presence, reachable vulnerable code, a vendor backport, accepted
residual risk and confirmed remediation are distinct conclusions. Unknown
reachability is not proof of absence. Confirm remediation with the relevant scan
and regression tests. Do not lower thresholds or add blanket ignores to pass CI.

## Verification checklist by risk

For every update, review source/manifest/lockfile diffs, pin consistency and release
notes; run checks relevant to affected code and preserve evidence/limitations.
Documentation-only changes need link/path/generation checks, not an automatic
full image rebuild. Routine library changes need affected tests, locked builds and
dependency scans. Broader runtime/compiler/SQLite changes need the complete candidate
validation and disposable-volume recovery checks. No test should use deployment data.

- Rust format/check/Clippy/tests/build; frontend locked install/typecheck/lint/tests/build
  for affected components, using the commands above and [README](README.md).
- Native process/browser tests when behavior or HTTP/assets change; see README.
- Compose configuration, image build, health/API/static assets, non-root/read-only,
  graceful shutdown and persistence tests for packaging/runtime changes.
- Migration review, fresh/existing database compatibility, verified backups and
  recovery for database/storage changes. For dependency/base upgrades with unchanged
  migration files, the existing [upgrade drill](scripts/sqlite-upgrade-smoke.py)
  accepts `--previous-image` and `--image` as documented in README; it is not rollback.
- RustSec, both npm scopes and the final-image scan as relevant. Review residual findings.
- Confirm all commands/scripts/docs belong to this application and CI has no required
  reference to the original starter. Record actual remote CI outcomes separately.

## Releases, Updates and Recovery

Use this manual runbook from the application's root on a single Docker host, in a
dedicated POSIX shell, with Docker/Compose v2, Git, curl and the tools in the scan
guide. Start from your own committed application repository. Commands below target
the generated single-instance Compose configuration and named SQLite volume. If
production uses extra Compose files, a bind mount, different database/port settings
or another project identity, preserve those settings and review the effective
configuration before adapting this example. Do not silently substitute a new volume.

### 1. Identify and retain the running release

`:local` is a mutable development tag. A unique release tag is a convention, not an
immutability guarantee. Docker's local image ID (`sha256:...`) identifies local
image content; a registry `repository@sha256:...` manifest digest is a different
identity used for distribution. No registry is required here. Record the running
container's `.Image`, not just the tag in Compose or the current Git checkout.

Set `PROJECT` to the **existing** Compose project identity (the generated name below
is the default, not permission to rename a deployment). Use your deployment records
and `docker compose ls` to confirm it. Keep HOST_PORT and all production overrides
unchanged. The preflight requires a clean Git working tree so the recorded revision identifies
the built source; commit reviewed non-secret configuration changes in your own
repository before a release. This example requires one running `app` container. If absent, inspect
the stopped container and deployment records instead of continuing these commands.

```sh
set -eu
umask 077
test -z "$(git status --porcelain)"
PROJECT=@NAME@
REVISION=$(git rev-parse HEAD)
RELEASE="${REVISION}-r1"
RECORD_DIR="../@NAME@-release-records/$RELEASE"
test ! -e "$RECORD_DIR"
mkdir -p "$RECORD_DIR"
CURRENT=$(docker compose -p "$PROJECT" -f compose.yaml ps -q app)
test -n "$CURRENT"
PREVIOUS_ID=$(docker inspect --format '{{.Image}}' "$CURRENT")
DATA_VOLUME=$(docker inspect --format '{{range .Mounts}}{{if eq .Destination "/data"}}{{.Name}}{{end}}{{end}}' "$CURRENT")
test -n "$DATA_VOLUME"
docker volume inspect "$DATA_VOLUME" --format '{{.Name}}'
PREVIOUS_TAG="@NAME@:retained-$RELEASE"
if docker image inspect "$PREVIOUS_TAG" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo 'Retained tag already exists; choose a new release identifier.' >&2
    exit 1
fi
docker image tag "$PREVIOUS_ID" "$PREVIOUS_TAG"
printf '%s\n' "source=$REVISION" "previous=$PREVIOUS_ID" "previous_container=$CURRENT" "retained=$PREVIOUS_TAG" "project=$PROJECT" "volume=$DATA_VOLUME" > "$RECORD_DIR/identity.txt"
```

Use an unused release suffix instead of `r1` for another build of the same commit;
do not overwrite retained tags or records. Retain previous image content locally
and in your chosen protected image archive/registry before pruning hosts. A local
tag is not protection against host loss or aggressive image pruning. Image identity
alone does not recover the database. The example records directory is outside the build context; choose another
protected writable location outside it if needed. Keep operational records out of build
contexts and public commits if they contain sensitive operational details.

### 2. Prepare and verify the candidate

Review source/manifest/lockfile changes and release notes. Run the Rust and frontend
checks in this guide/README and the dependency scans in SECURITY-SCANNING.md; stop
on failures. Review pending migration files against the running `_sqlx_migrations`
versions/checksums. Record whether an old binary can read **and start with** the
post-update database. When in doubt, test compatibility on an independent restored
copy before touching deployment data. Never test candidate migrations on production
merely to discover whether rollback works.

The following build uses normal caching. For OS package refreshes use the clean
build described earlier. In a managed TLS proxy supply the existing optional public
`proxy_ca` BuildKit secret; never disable TLS verification.

```sh
CANDIDATE_TAG="@NAME@:release-$RELEASE"
if docker image inspect "$CANDIDATE_TAG" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo 'Candidate tag already exists; choose a new release identifier.' >&2
    exit 1
fi
docker build --label "org.opencontainers.image.revision=$REVISION" -t "$CANDIDATE_TAG" .
CANDIDATE_ID=$(docker image inspect --format '{{.Id}}' "$CANDIDATE_TAG")
printf '%s\n' "candidate=$CANDIDATE_ID" "tag=$CANDIDATE_TAG" >> "$RECORD_DIR/identity.txt"
python3 scripts/docker-smoke.py --image "$CANDIDATE_ID" -v
python3 scripts/sqlite-backup-smoke.py --image "$CANDIDATE_ID" -v
python3 scripts/security-scan.py image --project . --image "$CANDIDATE_ID"
```

Those runners use their own disposable volumes, not deployment storage. A completed
image build is not a substitute for tests/scans. Backups, compatibility assessment,
known-image retention and data checks are essential safeguards. Browser/load tests
and wider platform coverage add assurance according to the change; they do not
replace data protection. Record every skipped check and its reason, rather than
calling an unchecked candidate production-ready.

For explicit local image selection, create the following operator-owned override
**once**, only if it does not already exist. On later releases inspect and reuse it;
never overwrite an existing override that contains other deployment settings.
`RELEASE_IMAGE` below is a Compose interpolation variable introduced by this file,
not an environment setting understood by the Rust application.

```sh
export RELEASE_IMAGE="$CANDIDATE_ID"
test ! -e release.compose.yaml
cat > release.compose.yaml <<'YAML'
services:
  app:
    image: "${RELEASE_IMAGE:?Set RELEASE_IMAGE to a verified local image ID}"
YAML
dc() { docker compose -p "$PROJECT" -f compose.yaml -f release.compose.yaml "$@"; }
dc config --quiet
```

The function exists only in this shell; later sessions must reconstruct/export the
recorded image ID and define it again. Always include this override when managing
the deployment; unqualified Compose would select `:local` again. Inspect the resolved
mounts/ports privately before deployment (full config may contain secrets). The
image is already local; deployment below forbids implicit builds or pulls.

### 3. Create and retain a consistent SQLite backup

The current API is read-only, but operators or a customized app may still write.
Quiesce **all** writers before the final pre-deployment snapshot, and keep them
quiesced through verification/cutover. For a writable application that cannot freeze
writes independently, stop it before running the backup helper. Otherwise a live
snapshot is consistent only at its snapshot point: later writes can be lost in
recovery. Do not promise a lossless rollback when writers cannot be controlled.

The helper supports online backup while readers/the app remain running. It uses
SQLite's backup API, finalizes WAL-independent output, checks integrity/FKs and
atomically refuses overwrite. Copying only an active `.db` can omit committed WAL
data. Do not manipulate live database/sidecar files manually.

```sh
BACKUP_VOLUME="${PROJECT}-backup-$RELEASE"
SNAPSHOT="pre-$RELEASE.sqlite"
if docker volume inspect "$BACKUP_VOLUME" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo 'Backup volume already exists; inspect it and choose an unused name.' >&2
    exit 1
fi
docker volume create "$BACKUP_VOLUME"
docker run --rm --read-only --no-healthcheck --network none --cap-drop ALL --security-opt no-new-privileges:true --tmpfs /tmp:size=32m,mode=1777 --mount "source=$DATA_VOLUME,target=/data" --mount "source=$BACKUP_VOLUME,target=/backups" --entrypoint /app/sqlite-backup "$PREVIOUS_ID" "$SNAPSHOT"
docker run --rm --read-only --network none --mount "source=$BACKUP_VOLUME,target=/backups,readonly" --entrypoint sqlite3 "$PREVIOUS_ID" -readonly "/backups/$SNAPSHOT" 'PRAGMA integrity_check; PRAGMA foreign_key_check; SELECT version,success,hex(checksum) FROM _sqlx_migrations ORDER BY version;' > "$RECORD_DIR/snapshot-checks.txt"
printf '%s\n' "backup_volume=$BACKUP_VOLUME" "snapshot=$SNAPSHOT" "snapshot_image=$PREVIOUS_ID" >> "$RECORD_DIR/identity.txt"
```

Inspect snapshot-checks.txt: integrity must be `ok`, foreign-key checking must have
no violation rows, and migration versions/success/checksums must match the expected
source history. Verify representative application data too; `ok` alone does not
prove the file contains the data you intended to preserve. Snapshot names must use
only the helper's permitted alphanumeric/dot/underscore/hyphen syntax.

Optionally export the **finalized snapshot**, not the live database. This helper
container never starts; remove only its recorded name afterwards. On an export
failure preserve the snapshot/volume and inspect before retrying.

```sh
EXPORT_CONTAINER="${PROJECT}-export-$RELEASE"
docker create --name "$EXPORT_CONTAINER" --read-only --network none --mount "source=$BACKUP_VOLUME,target=/backups,readonly" --entrypoint /bin/true "$PREVIOUS_ID"
docker cp "$EXPORT_CONTAINER:/backups/$SNAPSHOT" "$RECORD_DIR/$SNAPSHOT"
docker rm "$EXPORT_CONTAINER"
```

Protect exports and records with restrictive access; retain encrypted off-host
copies through your own backup system. A second local volume is independent of the
container but not of host loss. Do not commit database exports. A successful backup
command is **not** proof of recoverability: complete the next section first.

### 4. Prove recovery in an independent volume

For an incident in a new session, reconstruct PROJECT, backup volume, snapshot and
matching image from protected records. Do not infer the right version from a broken
current container. Choose unused restore/container names; never reuse an occupied
restore target. The recorded pre-update image is appropriate for its pre-update
snapshot; a newer image may apply additional migrations during validation.

```sh
RESTORE_VOLUME="${PROJECT}-restore-$RELEASE"
VALIDATION_CONTAINER="${PROJECT}-restore-check-$RELEASE"
RECOVERY_IMAGE="$PREVIOUS_ID"
if docker volume inspect "$RESTORE_VOLUME" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    echo 'Restore volume already exists; use a fresh target.' >&2
    exit 1
fi
docker volume create "$RESTORE_VOLUME"
docker run --rm --read-only --no-healthcheck --network none --cap-drop ALL --security-opt no-new-privileges:true --tmpfs /tmp:size=32m,mode=1777 --mount "source=$RESTORE_VOLUME,target=/data" --mount "source=$BACKUP_VOLUME,target=/backups,readonly" --entrypoint /app/sqlite-restore "$RECOVERY_IMAGE" "$SNAPSHOT"
docker run --rm --read-only --network none --mount "source=$RESTORE_VOLUME,target=/data,readonly" --entrypoint sqlite3 "$RECOVERY_IMAGE" -readonly /data/@NAME@.db 'PRAGMA integrity_check; PRAGMA foreign_key_check; SELECT version,success,hex(checksum) FROM _sqlx_migrations ORDER BY version;' > "$RECORD_DIR/restored-checks.txt"
diff "$RECORD_DIR/snapshot-checks.txt" "$RECORD_DIR/restored-checks.txt"
docker run -d --name "$VALIDATION_CONTAINER" --read-only --network none --cap-drop ALL --security-opt no-new-privileges:true --tmpfs /tmp:size=32m,mode=1777 --mount "source=$RESTORE_VOLUME,target=/data" "$RECOVERY_IMAGE"
docker exec "$VALIDATION_CONTAINER" curl --retry 30 --retry-delay 1 --retry-connrefused --fail --max-time 2 --silent --show-error http://127.0.0.1:8080/health/ready
docker exec "$VALIDATION_CONTAINER" curl --fail --silent --show-error http://127.0.0.1:8080/api/hello
docker stop -t 15 "$VALIDATION_CONTAINER"
docker inspect --format '{{.State.ExitCode}}' "$VALIDATION_CONTAINER"
docker rm "$VALIDATION_CONTAINER"
```

Require readiness, expected actual application data and clean exit code `0`. The
initial file inspection precedes application startup, while the restored file is
finalized without WAL sidecars. Once the app enables WAL, inspect through the app
or a normal writable data mount: a wholly read-only WAL directory without SHM can
fail to open even for a read-only SQL query. Do not change permissions or manipulate
sidecars to bypass that restriction. The
validation app has no published ports and mounts **only the restored data volume**;
it cannot open production storage. Preserve the original volume and the snapshot.
Helpers reject existing targets, unsafe symlinks and sidecars; do not bypass these
guards. Treat snapshots as trusted operator input, not arbitrary uploaded databases.

For eventual data cutover, stop the production and validation apps first. An
operator must review a persistent Compose volume mapping to the **verified restored
volume**, export the compatible image selection, validate the complete configuration
and verify service/data again. With the unchanged generated layout, review the
`volumes.light-data` definition: an external-volume declaration (`external: true`)
with `name` equal to the exact verified existing restore volume can retain the
service's `/data` mount without renaming PROJECT. Confirm the resolved mount before
starting; do not guess a new volume name or silently create empty storage.
This guide does not automatically switch volumes or
provide a universal cutover command for customized deployments. Preserve the
original volume until recovery is confirmed; do not run simultaneous instances
against it. Restoring a snapshot may discard later writes: decide whether they
must be reconciled before resuming service. Service availability is not the same
as recovery of the newest data.

### 5. Replace the application without replacing its data

Only after checks, compatible migration review, retained image and proven backup:

```sh
dc stop --timeout 15 app
dc up -d --no-build --pull never --wait --wait-timeout 60 app
CURRENT=$(dc ps -q app)
test "$(docker inspect --format '{{.Image}}' "$CURRENT")" = "$CANDIDATE_ID"
ACTUAL_VOLUME=$(docker inspect --format '{{range .Mounts}}{{if eq .Destination "/data"}}{{.Name}}{{end}}{{end}}' "$CURRENT")
test "$ACTUAL_VOLUME" = "$DATA_VOLUME"
curl --fail "http://127.0.0.1:${HOST_PORT:-8080}/health/ready"
curl --fail "http://127.0.0.1:${HOST_PORT:-8080}/api/hello"
docker exec "$CURRENT" sqlite3 -readonly /data/@NAME@.db 'SELECT version,success,hex(checksum) FROM _sqlx_migrations ORDER BY version;' > "$RECORD_DIR/deployed-migrations.txt"
printf '%s\n' "deployed=$CANDIDATE_ID" "container=$CURRENT" "data_volume=$ACTUAL_VOLUME" >> "$RECORD_DIR/identity.txt"
```

Compare expected data and migrations, not just HTTP 200. Record verification/time
and only then release your write freeze. This causes interruption; the existing
15-second container grace period exceeds the default ten-second app drain budget.
Startup migrations execute before traffic is served. A failed startup may still
have changed the database: preserve it and assess before retrying or reverting.

Never use `docker compose down -v`, volume removal, or global prune as an ordinary
upgrade/recovery step. Keep PROJECT and the actual data volume identity stable.
Do not assume changing the directory or project name finds the same database.

### 6. Application rollback: three compatibility cases

1. **No schema changes:** verify migration metadata and data semantics remain
   compatible. Exercise old/new versions on a disposable copy. Changed data formats
   can break rollback even without new SQL migrations.
2. **Backward-compatible migration:** additive SQL is not enough evidence. The old
   binary may reject migration versions absent from its embedded SQLx history.
   Verify startup, reads/writes and migration handling with the **actual old image**
   on an independent migrated copy. Never delete records or disable validation to
   simulate compatibility.
3. **Breaking/incompatible migration:** image-only rollback may fail or corrupt
   assumptions. Stop and plan coordinated application/data recovery, or a reviewed
   forward repair. Preserve the migrated original and all available writes. SQLx
   does not automatically reverse migrations; never edit deployed migration files.

Only if database compatibility with the retained image has been established:

```sh
docker image inspect "$PREVIOUS_ID" --format '{{.Id}}'
export RELEASE_IMAGE="$PREVIOUS_ID"
dc config --quiet
dc stop --timeout 15 app
dc up -d --no-build --pull never --wait --wait-timeout 60 app
CURRENT=$(dc ps -q app)
test "$(docker inspect --format '{{.Image}}' "$CURRENT")" = "$PREVIOUS_ID"
test "$(docker inspect --format '{{range .Mounts}}{{if eq .Destination "/data"}}{{.Name}}{{end}}{{end}}' "$CURRENT")" = "$DATA_VOLUME"
curl --fail "http://127.0.0.1:${HOST_PORT:-8080}/health/ready"
curl --fail "http://127.0.0.1:${HOST_PORT:-8080}/api/hello"
printf '%s\n' "rolled_back_to=$PREVIOUS_ID" >> "$RECORD_DIR/identity.txt"
```

Image rollback retains the current database; it does not restore snapshot data.
Record the result and any ongoing incident. Do not repeat deployment blindly if
health/data checks fail. If the old image is missing, stop and recover the exact
trusted artifact from your archive/registry or prepare a separately verified build;
a rebuild of old source against live package repositories need not be identical.

### 7. Failure decisions

| Failure | Check/preserve | Safe next decision |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Candidate build fails | Build phase, lockfiles, tool pins, CA/registry access, free disk; keep current image/data | Fix candidate in isolation; do not replace the running service |
| Startup fails | Locally inspect `docker compose` status/logs using the same project/files; retain failed container metadata and database | Assess configuration, permissions and migrations before retry/rollback |
| Health fails | Process state, readiness DB query, storage, migration status; preserve data | Stop replacement attempts; health failure alone does not prove image rollback safe |
| Migration fails | Existing versions/checksums, SQLx diagnostics, free space; retain original/history | Never delete migration records/files or reset the volume; determine forward repair versus compatible restore |
| Expected data is absent | Actual /data mount, project name, image and DB path | Stop writers; do not seed/reset or create another guessed volume |
| Previous image missing | Recorded ID, retained tag and trusted archive | No guaranteed rollback; verify a recovered/rebuilt artifact independently |
| Backup invalid | Artifact identity, integrity/FK/data/history checks; preserve other backups and original DB | Do not deploy relying on it; obtain a valid backup or stop for recovery planning |
| Restore fails | UID/GID, empty target, canonical paths, sidecars, local hard-link support and matching image | Keep original/snapshot untouched; diagnose and use another fresh restore target |
| Volume identity changed | Effective Compose project/files/mount; preserve both old and unexpected volumes | Stop writes and reconcile identity before any container replacement |
| Old image rejects migrated DB | Actual embedded migrations and data compatibility | Image-only rollback is insufficient; plan coordinated recovery/forward repair |

Read logs privately using the same Compose files/project (for example `dc logs
--tail 100 app` once the function/image environment is reconstructed). Never publish
raw config, secrets, database exports or arbitrary logs as troubleshooting artifacts.
A backup restore test, a health check and a passing scanner each provide different,
limited evidence; none guarantees production readiness or lossless disaster recovery.
